From: SoundTap contributors <soundtap@localhost>
Subject: [PATCH] Catch up a late pipe read instead of resyncing

When a read finishes after its scheduled tick, AsioStream resyncs: it
restarts the schedule from now. The time it was late is never caught up,
so on a busy speaker (an ST10 leading a group runs at ~85 % CPU) the
reader runs about 1 % slower than the writer. The pipe backlog grows to
the pipe size, and the writer, which must not block the DAC, drops
audio that every follower then hears as a click.

Read the backlog right away when the read is late by less than a second
and keep the schedule. Longer gaps still resync, as before.

Upstream: Not yet submitted
Signed-off-by: Stein Milder <info@steinmilder.nl>

---
--- a/server/streamreader/asio_stream.hpp
+++ b/server/streamreader/asio_stream.hpp
@@ -269,6 +269,14 @@
             });
             return;
         }
+        // Late by less than a second: the audio we were late for is still in the pipe, so
+        // read it now and keep the schedule. A resync restarts the schedule from now, so
+        // the time we were late is never caught up; on a busy speaker the backlog grows
+        // until the pipe is full and the writer drops audio.
+        else if (currentTick - nextTick_ < std::chrono::seconds(1))
+        {
+            do_read();
+        }
         // Read took longer, wait for the buffer to fill up
         else
         {
